    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Research problem</h2>

      <p>
        Extracapsular extension occurs when cancer spreads beyond the
        lymph-node capsule. It is an important prognostic factor in head and
        neck cancer and can influence staging, prognosis, and treatment
        planning. Preoperative assessment from CT remains difficult because
        the radiologic signs may be subtle, heterogeneous, and localized
        around the nodal boundary, while pathology-confirmed labels are
        limited.
      </p>

      <p>
        Conventional models may learn from the complete CT volume, including
        anatomically unrelated structures, or use segmentation only as a
        preprocessing or auxiliary task. Both strategies leave the classifier
        vulnerable to irrelevant background features and provide a weak
        connection between localization and the final clinical prediction.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Knowledge-informed constrained learning</h2>

      <p>
        The framework uses a shared dual-output 3D SwinUNETR to jointly predict
        the lymph-node region and classify ECE. Anatomical supervision
        therefore shapes the latent representation rather than serving only
        as a separate preprocessing step.
      </p>

      <p>
        The predicted anatomical mask is projected into the latent feature
        space and normalized into soft spatial weights. These weights
        determine which features reach the classifier, emphasizing nodal and
        perinodal evidence while suppressing unrelated background anatomy.
        The model consequently learns both where to look and which evidence
        should support the final decision.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Clinical prior and data-efficient learning</h2>

      <p>
        Because ECE is defined by spread beyond the lymph-node capsule, the
        supervision target is expanded to include clinically plausible
        perinodal soft tissue while excluding implausible regions such as air
        and bone. This incorporates the disease mechanism directly into model
        training.
      </p>

      <p>
        To address scarce pathology-confirmed labels, the encoder is
        initialized through Mask-in-Mask self-supervised pretraining on
        approximately 1,500 unlabeled head-and-neck CT volumes. The
        pretraining stage learns anatomical representations at multiple
        spatial scales before supervised fine-tuning on the ECE cohort.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Selected results</h2>

      <p>
        The study evaluated 150 patients with pathology-confirmed ECE status.
        The proposed model achieved:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Accuracy: 0.924</li>
        <li>AUC: 0.928</li>
        <li>Sensitivity: 0.926</li>
        <li>Specificity: 0.892</li>
        <li>Lymph-node ROI segmentation Dice: 0.821</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The model achieved the strongest accuracy, AUC, sensitivity, and Dice
        among the evaluated methods. During inference, it predicts the
        anatomical region directly from the CT volume and uses that prediction
        to guide classification, so no manually supplied ROI is required for
        the final decision.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Interpretability and decision support</h2>

      <p>
        The soft spatial weights are part of the model's actual decision
        pathway, not a post hoc explanation. They show where the classifier
        aggregates evidence, while Grad-CAM provides a complementary analysis
        of which regions influence the final prediction. Together with the
        predicted anatomical mask, these outputs make it possible to examine
        whether the decision is supported by clinically meaningful nodal and
        perinodal evidence.
      </p>
    </section>

